Current State
This tenant has no functioning lifecycle governance. Twenty-six of 104 groups have no owner, every Team permits guest access, anyone in the organization can invite external users, and no group expiration policy exists. The controls that are in place — Teams meeting and messaging policies, cross-tenant access configuration, and SharePoint site label coverage — are structural defaults and do not substitute for the ownership, review, and retirement processes that are absent. The tenant is not ready for expanded Copilot deployment until the ownership and external-access gaps below are closed.

The findings below are drawn entirely from the tenant telemetry collected by this assessment. Sections are grouped by governance domain. Each finding states what was confirmed, the scale of the gap, and the consequence.

Domain 01
Ownership & Accountability
NO-GO
26 Groups Have No Owner
The governance:ownerless-groups check returned ownerlessGroupCount: 26 out of 104 total groups. The affected groups include production-named workspaces — Finance, Sales, Legal, Payroll, Billing, Human Solutions, Managed Services, and others — alongside MSP client-named groups (Barricade MSP, Columbia River Carbonates, Roofing CRM, Terminal Transfer, LifeWorksNW, Hunter Davisson, Netropole, ATRIO, TARR, Vigilant, and more).

A group with no owner has no accountable party to approve membership changes, review guest access, or authorize deletion. Microsoft's built-in recovery path for ownerless groups relies on periodic owner-notification emails — those emails currently go nowhere. Any member of these groups can change membership without oversight, and the groups cannot be reviewed, recertified, or retired under any formal process.

PASS
All 18 Teams Report an Owner
teams:ownerless-teams returned ownerlessTeamCount: 0. Every one of the 18 Teams has at least one designated owner. This is the tenant's strongest governance signal. Note that many of these Teams correspond to groups in the ownerless-groups list above — the Teams ownership record is separate from the group ownership record; verify that the same individuals are recorded as group owners where both objects exist.

WARNING
All 104 Groups Are Manually Managed — No Dynamic Membership Rules Exist
governance:dynamic-group-usage shows dynamicGroupCount: 0 and membershipRule_count: 0 across all 104 groups. Every group membership is maintained by hand. When a user changes role, department, or employment status, no group membership updates automatically. This makes stale access the default outcome rather than the exception, and it places the entire membership-accuracy burden on manual administrator action with no enforcement backstop.

Domain 02
Lifecycle — Creation, Review & Retirement
NO-GO
No Group Expiration Policy Is Configured
governance:group-expiration-policy returned groupExpirationPolicyConfigured: false with zero policies recorded. Groups and their associated SharePoint sites, mailboxes, and Planner boards have no automated retirement trigger. Once created, a group persists indefinitely unless an administrator manually deletes it. Combined with the ownerless-groups finding above, this means there is no mechanism — automatic or ownership-driven — by which an unneeded group will ever be retired.

WARNING
99 SharePoint Sites Flagged for Inactivity — Last-Activity Dates Unavailable
sharepoint:inactive-sites identified 99 sites but returned inactiveSiteCount: 0 and lastModifiedDateTime_count: 0. The activity-date field did not populate, so definitive inactive status cannot be confirmed from this scan. The list of site IDs is present and spans both mccawsoft2.sharepoint.com and mccawsoft2-my.sharepoint.com (personal OneDrive) namespaces.

The data gap itself is a governance problem: without readable activity dates, there is no basis for a lifecycle decision on these 99 sites. A follow-up pull against the SharePoint admin center activity reports is required before any retirement action can be taken.

WARNING
27 Channels Across 18 Teams — Naming Inconsistency and Test Channels Present
teams:channel-sprawl returned channelCount: 27. Among the channel display names: "Test", "Test 123", "Test 12345" — three channels with test names that have not been removed. Sixteen of the 27 channels are named "General" (the default), meaning almost no custom channel structure exists. The tenant has not established a naming convention for channels, and test artifacts have persisted into production inventory.

Domain 03
Sharing & External Access
NO-GO
Any User Can Invite External Guests — No Invite Restriction Configured
identity:b2b-collaboration-settings returned allowInvitesFrom: everyone and guestInviteRestriction: everyone. Any licensed user in this tenant can add an external guest without administrator approval. For an MSP tenant that manages client environments, this means any internal staff member — or a compromised internal account — can introduce an external identity with no checkpoint. The consequence is that the guest roster has no authoritative owner and cannot be validated against a known-approved list.

NO-GO
24 Guest Accounts Exist With No Access Review Program
governance:guest-count shows 24 guest account objects. governance:guest-access-reviews returned guestAccessReviewExists: false — no access review schedule has ever been configured for guest accounts. governance:guest-staleness shows all 24 accounts but returned no sign-in activity data (signInActivity_count: 0), so the last sign-in for any guest is unknown from this scan.

These 24 accounts have open, unreviewed access to whatever groups and Teams they were added to. None of them have been certified by any review process. The missing sign-in telemetry means dormant guests cannot be identified without a separate sign-in log query. The security consequence is persistent external access with no expiry and no accountability.

WARNING
Every Team in the Tenant Contains Guest Members
teams:guest-membership returned teamsWithGuestsCount: 18, which equals the total Teams inventory of 18. Every Team — including client-named Teams such as ATRIO, Roofing CRM, TARR, Terminal Transfer, and the template Teams — has at least one external guest. Given the open invite policy and absent review program, there is no established process by which a guest's continued presence in any of these Teams would be challenged.

PASS
Cross-Tenant Access Policy Is Configured
identity:cross-tenant-access returned crossTenantAccessConfigured: true. A cross-tenant access policy exists. This is a positive control but does not compensate for the open B2B invite setting — the cross-tenant policy governs trust relationships between specific tenants, while the B2B invite setting governs who can initiate invitations from within this tenant. Both controls must be set correctly; one passing does not remediate the other.

Domain 04
Access Reviews & Role Hygiene
NO-GO
No Access Reviews Configured — Guest or Otherwise
governance:guest-access-reviews returned guestAccessReviewExists: false with zero review schedules. governance:overdue-access-reviews returned overdueAccessReviewCount: 0 — not because reviews are current, but because no review program exists to generate overdue items.

There is no periodic certification of who has access to what. No group membership, no guest account, and no privileged assignment in this tenant is subject to a formal review cycle. For an MSP managing client data, the absence of access reviews is a direct compliance gap — most recognized frameworks (SOC 2, ISO 27001, NIST CSF) require periodic access recertification as a named control.

WARNING
Sign-In Activity Unreadable — Stale Account Status Cannot Be Confirmed
identity:stale-accounts identified 24 account objects but returned staleAccountCount: 0 and signInActivity_count: 0. The last sign-in timestamp did not populate for any of these 24 accounts. These are the same 24 account IDs that appear in the guest-count and guest-staleness checks. The telemetry gap means neither active nor inactive status can be confirmed from this scan. Sign-in logs must be queried directly from Entra ID to determine which of these accounts are dormant.

Domain 05
Structure, Naming & Policy Enforcement
PASS
Meeting and Messaging Policies Assigned to All 18 Teams
teams:meeting-policy-coverage returned meetingPolicyAssignedCount: 18 and teams:messaging-policy-coverage returned messagingPolicyAssignedCount: 18. Every Team carries a meeting and messaging policy assignment. This confirms policy coverage at the Teams layer is complete.

WARNING
One Team Is Public — "Test NASA"
teams:inventory-count returned publicTeamCount: 1 of 18 Teams. The public Team is "Test NASA" (visibility confirmed as Public in the visibility values, with all other 17 Teams as Private). A Public Team is discoverable and joinable by any user in the tenant without owner approval. For a client-facing MSP workspace named with a client identifier, public visibility is inconsistent with the access control model applied to the other 17 Teams.

PASS
All 99 SharePoint Sites Carry a Sensitivity Label
sharepoint:site-label-coverage returned sitesWithLabelCount: 99 against a site population of 99. Every enumerated site has a label applied. Label quality — whether labels are correctly scoped and not set to an overly permissive value — is addressed in the separate compliance and data-protection report; this finding confirms structural coverage only.

WARNING
No Consistent Naming Convention Evident Across 104 Groups
The governance:ownerless-groups display names show a mix of personal mailbox groups (prefixed "SMC —" with user email addresses and personal names), client-named groups, department names, test labels ("test12345", "Trial", "License Test -- Shane"), and system groups (e.g. "Group for Answers in Viva Engage – DO NOT DELETE"). No enforced naming prefix or policy schema is evident. Without a naming convention policy in Entra ID, group creation is unconstrained, and the inventory cannot be audited by type, owner, or purpose without manual inspection.

Domain 06
Platform Change Awareness
REVIEW REQUIRED
440 Message Center Items — 108 Major Changes Pending Review
m365:message-center returned _itemCount: 440 total items, of which majorChangeCount: 108. Categories include planForChange, preventOrFixIssue, and stayInformed items. Governance-relevant items in the queue include: EWS retirement (requiring action on calendar and mailbox integrations), SharePoint Restricted Search retirement, SharePoint Designer 2013 end-of-support in July 2026, Remote Event Receivers retirement, legacy whiteboard retirement, Teams Live Events retirement, Turnitin Similarity integration retirement, and Microsoft Publisher end-of-support in October 2026.

This report does not triage all 440 items — that work belongs to a dedicated change-management review. The governance relevance here is structural: 108 major change notifications represent decisions that must be assigned to an accountable owner and tracked to resolution. Without a named owner for each change item, decisions on tenant configuration changes default to inaction, which is itself a governance failure. The preventOrFixIssue category items carry the highest urgency and must be triaged first.

Required Actions
What to Fix, In Order of Impact
Ordered by what each fix actually closes. Each action maps directly to a confirmed finding above.

#
Action
Severity
01
Restrict guest invitation to administrators only
Change allowInvitesFrom from everyone to adminsAndGuestInviters or adminsOnly in the Entra External Identities settings. This closes the uncontrolled guest introduction path immediately. No review or planning is required; it is a single setting change.
CRITICAL
02
Configure a recurring access review for all 24 guest accounts
Create an Entra ID Access Review scoped to guest accounts, set to quarterly recurrence, with group owners as reviewers. Before the first review completes, pull Entra sign-in logs to identify guests with no sign-in in the past 90 days and disable those accounts immediately. This closes the unreviewed external access gap confirmed by governance:guest-access-reviews.
CRITICAL
03
Assign owners to all 26 ownerless groups
Export the 26 ownerless group IDs from governance:ownerless-groups and assign a named human owner to each. Groups that cannot be assigned an owner within 30 days should be evaluated for deletion. This is a prerequisite for the expiration policy (action 04) to function — an expiration policy notifies owners, so ownerless groups bypass the retirement workflow entirely.
CRITICAL
04
Configure a Microsoft 365 group expiration policy
Enable a group expiration policy in Entra ID (Entra Admin Center → Groups → Expiration). A 180-day lifetime with owner-renewal notification is a reasonable starting point. After action 03 is complete and all groups have owners, this policy will trigger automatic retirement of unused groups rather than relying on manual cleanup. Without this, the group inventory has no self-correcting mechanism. This closes the gap confirmed by governance:group-expiration-policy.
HIGH
05
Resolve the SharePoint site activity data gap and complete a lifecycle review
Pull last-modified and last-activity dates for all 99 sites directly from the SharePoint admin center activity report. Sites inactive for more than 180 days with no owner response should be archived or deleted. The sharepoint:inactive-sites scan could not confirm activity dates — this requires a separate data pull before any deletion decision is made.
HIGH
06
Change "Test NASA" from Public to Private and remove test channels
Set the "Test NASA" Team visibility to Private in the Teams admin center. Delete the three test channels ("Test", "Test 123", "Test 12345") from Teams inventory. These are confirmed from teams:inventory-count and teams:channel-sprawl. Neither action requires planning — both are immediate cleanup items.
MEDIUM
07
Triage Message Center preventOrFixIssue items and assign owners to all 108 major changes
From the m365:message-center queue of 440 items (108 major changes), filter for preventOrFixIssue category first and assign each a named owner and target resolution date. EWS retirement, SharePoint Designer end-of-support, and Teams Live Events retirement are among the items with confirmed retirement dates that require configuration decisions before their deadlines.
MEDIUM
08
Implement a group naming policy in Entra ID
Enable the Entra ID group naming policy with a prefix that encodes group purpose or ownership type (e.g. CLIENT-, INTERNAL-, MSP-). This does not rename existing groups but applies to all new group creation going forward and eliminates the current inconsistency confirmed across the 104-group inventory in governance:ownerless-groups.
LOW
